{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfk5wrctD7QymnAwUXvK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't reliably extract Dq and B from these three bands, so I'm not reporting numbers for them.\n\n1. **Origin of the bands.** The ground configuration of low-spin d⁵ is t₂g⁵, and it gives only one term, ²T₂g. There are no excited doublets inside t₂g⁵. Every spin-allowed d–d band therefore comes from t₂g⁴eg¹ doublets, which lie about 10Dq above the ground state. These doublets are ²A₁g, ²A₂g, ²Eg (two), ²T₁g (three) and ²T₂g (three), and several are close together.\n\n2. **No unique assignment.** The three observed bands are at 22 640, 22 930 and 25 080 cm⁻¹. The first two are only 290 cm⁻¹ apart. Nothing in the data says which of the many closely spaced t₂g⁴eg¹ doublets each band is. Each choice of assignment gives a different (Dq, B) pair. Fixing C/B = 4.6 leaves two unknowns, but the assignment ambiguity means the fit is under-determined.\n\n3. **Charge transfer.** In real low-spin d⁵ complexes such as [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻, ligand-to-metal charge-transfer bands usually overlap or hide the weak doublet–doublet d–d bands. Bands in this region may not be pure d–d transitions at all.\n\nA fit would need a firm term assignment for each band, for example from band intensities, polarization or band widths. It would also need the t₂g⁴eg¹ doublet energies from the full d⁵ Tanabe–Sugano matrices.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"Low-spin d5 d-d bands are all t2g4eg1 doublets with no unique assignment of the three bands (and LMCT overlap), so Dq and B are not reliably determinable.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":826,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":826,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard